     5	<chapter id="ma-manual" xreflabel="MemAccess">
     6	
     7	<title>MemAccess: memory access pattern profiling</title>
     8	
     9	<para>To use this tool, you must specify
    10	<option>--tool=memaccess</option> on the Valgrind command line.</para>
    11	
    12	<para>MemAccess is useful when Memcheck reports many invalid accesses and
    13	the individual errors do not make the underlying traversal pattern obvious.
    14	It records data loads and stores, attributes them to the guest function
    15	containing the accessing instruction, and summarizes how each function
    16	walks through memory.</para>
    17	
    18	<sect1 id="ma-manual.overview" xreflabel="Overview">
    19	<title>Overview</title>
    20	
    21	<para>For every function, MemAccess reports the number of reads and writes,
    22	the most common address stride, and one of these access patterns:</para>
    23	
    24	<variablelist>
    25	
    26	  <varlistentry>
    27	    <term><computeroutput>sequential</computeroutput></term>
    28	    <listitem>
    29	      <para>At least <option>--sequential-threshold</option> percent of
    30	      consecutive accesses in that direction advance by exactly the access
    31	      size, either forwards or backwards.</para>
    32	    </listitem>
    33	  </varlistentry>
    34	
    35	  <varlistentry>
    36	    <term><computeroutput>strided</computeroutput></term>
    37	    <listitem>
    38	      <para>At least <option>--sequential-threshold</option> percent of
    39	      consecutive accesses use one constant stride that is not equal to the
    40	      access size.  This includes repeated accesses to one address
    41	      (stride zero).  For example, reading every second element of an array
    42	      produces a strided pattern.</para>
    43	    </listitem>
    44	  </varlistentry>
    45	
    46	  <varlistentry>
    47	    <term><computeroutput>random</computeroutput></term>
    48	    <listitem>
    49	      <para>Neither of the conditions above is met.  This includes pointer
    50	      chasing, hash-table walks, and interleaved traversals that frequently
    51	      switch between unrelated addresses.</para>
    52	    </listitem>
    53	  </varlistentry>
    54	
    55	  <varlistentry>
    56	    <term><computeroutput>n/a</computeroutput></term>
    57	    <listitem>
    58	      <para>The function has fewer than two accesses in that direction, so
    59	      no stride can be calculated.</para>
    60	    </listitem>
    61	  </varlistentry>
    62	
    63	</variablelist>
    64	
    65	<para>Reads and writes are tracked independently.  Read-modify-write
    66	operations count as both one read and one write.  Instruction fetches are
    67	not included.  The stride is the difference between consecutive data
    68	addresses observed for the same function and direction.  Compute the
    69	pattern separately for each function so that unrelated functions do not
    70	contaminate each other's streams.</para>
    71	
    72	</sect1>
    73	
    74	<sect1 id="ma-manual.output" xreflabel="Output">
    75	<title>Output</title>
    76	
    77	<para>A summary table is printed on Valgrind's normal output channel.  For
    78	example:</para>
    79	
    80	<programlisting>
    81	Function                         Reads       Writes  R-stride  R-pattern  W-stride  W-pattern
    82	parse_packet                        4096         1024         4 sequential        -        n/a
    83	lookup_chain                         731            0        16    strided        -        n/a
    84	</programlisting>
    85	
    86	<para>MemAccess also writes folded stack data for flame graph tools.  The
    87	data is written to <computeroutput>memaccess.out.%p</computeroutput> by
    88	default, where <computeroutput>%p</computeroutput> is the process ID.  Each
    89	line has the form:</para>
    90	
    91	<programlisting>
    92	memaccess;READ;parse_packet;sequential 4096
    93	memaccess;WRITE;parse_packet;sequential 1024
    94	</programlisting>
    95	
    96	<para>The counts are memory access counts.  Function names are used as
    97	frames; this tool intentionally does not unwind the call stack.  This makes
    98	the data cheap to collect and keeps attribution stable even when debug
    99	information for callers is incomplete.  Feed the file directly to
   100	<computeroutput>flamegraph.pl</computeroutput>, for example:</para>
   101	
   102	<programlisting>
   103	flamegraph.pl memaccess.out.12345 &gt; memaccess.svg
   104	</programlisting>
   105	
   106	<para>Function names have whitespace and semicolons replaced with
   107	underscores so that they remain valid folded-stack frame names.</para>
   108	
   109	</sect1>
   110	
   111	<sect1 id="ma-manual.options" xreflabel="MemAccess Command-line Options">
   112	<title>MemAccess Command-line Options</title>
   113	
   114	<para>MemAccess-specific command-line options are:</para>
   115	
   116	<!-- start of xi:include in the manpage -->
   117	<variablelist id="ma.opts.list">
   118	
   119	  <varlistentry id="opt.flamegraph-out-file" xreflabel="--flamegraph-out-file">
   120	    <term>
   121	      <option><![CDATA[--flamegraph-out-file=<file> [default: memaccess.out.%p] ]]></option>
   122	    </term>
   123	    <listitem>
   124	      <para>Write folded flame graph data to <filename>file</filename>.
   125	      The usual Valgrind file-name substitutions, including
   126	      <computeroutput>%p</computeroutput>, are supported.</para>
   127	    </listitem>
   128	  </varlistentry>
   129	
   130	  <varlistentry id="opt.sequential-threshold" xreflabel="--sequential-threshold">
   131	    <term>
   132	      <option><![CDATA[--sequential-threshold=<0..100> [default: 90] ]]></option>
   133	    </term>
   134	    <listitem>
   135	      <para>Set the minimum percentage of transitions that must have a
   136	      particular stride before a stream is classified as sequential or
   137	      strided.  Lower values classify more streams as regular; higher values
   138	      require a stronger pattern.</para>
   139	    </listitem>
   140	  </varlistentry>
   141	
   142	</variablelist>
   143	<!-- end of xi:include in the manpage -->
   144	
   145	</sect1>
   146	
   147	<sect1 id="ma-manual.limitations" xreflabel="Limitations">
   148	<title>Limitations</title>
   149	
   150	  <para>MemAccess observes guest virtual addresses.  It does not account for
   151	physical-page mapping, and Valgrind's own address layout can differ from a
   152	native run.  The pattern is based on consecutive accesses in program order
   153	within one function; it does not reconstruct higher-level objects or loop
   154	nesting.  Accesses to multiple interleaved buffers in one function share
   155	the same stream and may therefore be classified as random.  Multi-threaded
   156	executions also share one stream per function, so interleaving by different
   157	threads can make a regular stream appear random.</para>
   158	
   159	<para>The tool is substantially slower than native execution because every
   160	load and store calls a helper.  Use it after Memcheck has identified the
   161	interesting part of a program, or on a reduced reproduction.</para>
   162	
   163	<para>As with other Valgrind tools, VEX optimisation can remove guest loads
   164	whose results are dead.  Such accesses are not observable by MemAccess at
   165	the default optimisation level.  Use
   166	<option>--vex-iropt-level=0</option> when an exhaustive raw load trace is
   167	required.</para>
   168	
   169	</sect1>
